8. Python Validation Code
python
from sympy import isprime

def get_SI(limit):
    excluded = set()
    for x in range(1, limit+1):
        for y in range(1, limit+1):
            v1 = 2*x*y + x + y
            v2 = 2*x*y + x + y - 1
            if v1 <= limit: excluded.add(v1)
            if v2 <= limit: excluded.add(v2)
    return [m for m in range(1, limit+1) if m not in excluded]

SI = get_SI(10000)
# Verify bijection
assert all(isprime(2*s+1) and isprime(2*s+3) for s in SI)
# Verify Modular Resonance (one exception at SI(1)=1)
assert all(s % 3 == 2 for s in SI[1:])
# Verify Gap Divisibility
assert all((SI[i+1]-SI[i]) % 3 == 0 for i in range(1, len(SI)-1))
# Verify Interval Conjecture
assert all(SI[i+1] < 2*SI[i]**2 + 4*SI[i] + 1 for i in range(len(SI)-1))
print("All theorems verified.")
9. Conclusions
The Twin Prime Sieve framework provides the cleanest formalization of TPC: the conjecture is equivalent to the infinitude of the sieve integer sequence SI. Four theorems are proven unconditionally. The Modular Resonance Theorem reduces the search space by 67%, channeling all twin primes (beyond the (3,5) exception) into the arithmetic progression {3k-1 : k ∈ ℤ⁺}. The TP-SI Interval Conjecture is verified computationally but remains logically equivalent to TPC.

The parity barrier prevents proving |SI| = ∞ using current sieve methods. The Singular Insight — a technique for simultaneously forcing both 2S+1 and 2S+3 prime across the mod-3 corridor — remains the open frontier.

Zero-Error status: All proven results are exact. All conditional results are labelled. The conjecture stands as a coherent prediction; its proof remains open.

